{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>For every man, every man who curses.</b> It is difficult that it says <b>ki</b>, which gives a reason for what precedes it, yet it said nothing before this that could be its reason. And if it refers to the observance of the statutes — is only the cursing of parents included in this verse, that it was fitting to say ki? Further, why did it repeat saying ״אִישׁ אִישׁ״? And in Torat Kohanim they said: ״אִישׁ״ — I have only a man; whence a woman? Scripture says ״אִישׁ אִישׁ״ — end of quote. It is difficult: whence would it enter one's mind to exclude a woman, that an inclusion was necessary, when surely Scripture equated woman to man for all punishments in the Torah as a general rule, as they said, of blessed memory (Bava Kamma 15a). And if it was because Scripture was concerned to explain אִישׁ as coming to exclude a woman, it should not have written אִישׁ, which would mislead one to say that it comes to exclude a woman, and instead should have said ״אָדָם כִּי יְקַלֵּל״ etc. Further, why did it repeat saying ״אָבִיו וְאִמּוֹ קִלֵּל״?" "Now the intent of Scripture is as follows: because it had commanded beforehand regarding passing one's son or daughter to Molech, and Scripture was particular only about his own seed, whereas another's child, and even his relatives such as his brother and sister, are exempt — as is taught in Sanhedrin (Sanhedrin 64b), and this is their language: he is liable only for those who issue from his thigh, etc., end of quote. And one must explain the reason for his liability regarding his son and daughter but not regarding others' children — which is because his children are judged as a person's property; therefore his actions are effective when he offers them to idolatry by passing them to Molech, but regarding anyone else his actions make no impression, on the principle of their statement, of blessed memory (Arakhin 28a, Bava Metzia 7a), even regarding consecrated items of Heaven: a person cannot consecrate something that is not his. And from this a person might say: but surely the son is not entirely the father's either, as they said, of blessed memory (Niddah 31a), that the father and the mother are partners in the person — the white from the father and the red from the mother; and if so, by this very reason the father would not be liable for the son on account of the mother's portion. Therefore Scripture said, after the warning and the punishment for passing his children to Molech: ״כִּי אִישׁ אִישׁ אֲשֶׁר יְקַלֵּל״ etc. ״מוֹת יוּמָת״ — and it was precise in saying ״אֶת אָבִיו וְאֶת אִמּוֹ״, meaning, this one independently or this one independently, as they deduced in Torat Kohanim regarding this very verse, that there is liability for each one of the two. Behold, the son is put to death because he offended against his father or against his mother's honor, and we do not say: but the son is not entirely the father's or the mother's, to put him to death on their account." "And if you say: I am also judging this — why should this be the law? — therefore it concludes saying <b>his father and his mother he cursed</b>, meaning that when he curses his father he also curses his mother with him, and likewise the reverse. And the reason is because the father and the mother are one body, as they said, of blessed memory (Berakhot 24a): his wife is like his body. And accordingly, whether the act is done against one of them, or the act is done from one of them toward their children, it is considered as done to both of them together. And from this you will find that HaShem punishes a man by killing his wife, as it is written (Proverbs 22:27): ״לָמָּה יִקַּח מִשְׁכָּבְךָ מִתַּחְתֶּיךָ״." "And it repeated saying <b>אִישׁ אִישׁ</b> because we find that Scripture attached males to females and females to males, and they said, of blessed memory (Niddah 31a): a woman who emits seed first gives birth to a male. If so, there is a difference between the birth of a male and the birth of a female, and from this side the distinction in the nature of the parents changes, and there is room to say — because Scripture attached males to females — that fathers would not be put to death for passing sons, or mothers for passing daughters, since Scripture's attachment was to the fathers; therefore Scripture says ״אִישׁ אִישׁ״ — one for himself and one for the woman, for both of them are subject to the liability of passing a son or a daughter." "<b>Further,</b> it appears that because Scripture said ״אָבִיו וְאִמּוֹ״, and our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted (Yalkut Shimoni 619) that ״אָבִיו״ comes to exclude his father's father, and ״אִמּוֹ״ comes to exclude his mother's mother — that they are not put to death — therefore Scripture was concerned lest one err and say that the exclusion applies specifically as ״אָבִיו״ and not her father, ״אִמּוֹ״ and not her mother, and if so women would be exempt from this punishment; therefore Scripture says ״אִישׁ אִישׁ״ to include a woman."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {2 "The Proverbs verse is cited in rabbinic literature (e.g., Sanhedrin 100a) as a warning of divine punishment through one's wife; the Or HaChaim applies it here to the concept of spousal unity."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:00:13.613196348Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}